+# Six drafting objections recovered; the 693 LHM adds visible clauses but not an authenticated attachment link
+
+**Exhibit 349 supplies Jones's six substantive comments that were missing from the preceding comparison.** They can now be compared with the six responses in 350. Exhibit 693 supplies a readable six-page LHM containing several relevant clauses. Its author/version/attachment relationship still needs authentication: neither a shared date/topic nor a matching paragraph proves it is the exact draft criticized in 349 or the revised attachment referenced in 350.
+
+## What the newly read sources contain
+
+[349](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/349.pdf), physical page 1, is a Jones-to-Maltbie message displayed **8/28/01 11:51AM**, subject **Re: ZM Fisa**, with a quoted Maltbie message displayed **08/28/01 10:05AM**. The quoted message says the LHM is attached for comments and that the Al-Khattab/UBL foreign-power information will be added later, **“when we get an atty to buy this argument.”** Jones's first item questions that sequencing and says it seems they are **“setting this up for failure”** if the connection is not firmly established for initial review. These are contemporaneous statements of proposed sequencing and concern, not our finding that evidence was deliberately suppressed or that a particular reviewer received an incomplete package.
+
+[693](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/693.pdf) is **six pages**, with an opening date **August 24, 2001** and Minneapolis origin/request language. The court index attributes drafting to Maltbie; the visible body does not contain a native authoring history or an explicit link to either August 28 attachment. All six pages were read. The request seeks emergency search authority for personal effects/property and a residence, with the concluding request spanning physical pages **5–6**. Urgency and uncertainty about plan extent/associates remain in the final paragraph. A request is not an issued warrant or a completed search.
+
+## The six comments, responses and held text
+
+Each row pairs **349's numbered comment with 350's numbered response**. Their matching six-item structure, participants and topics support a source-level comment/response association. Native thread/attachment metadata, the exact questioned draft and the final revised text remain separate missing inputs. Locators below are physical PDF pages; 347's LHM is within its larger nine-page packet.
+
+| Item | 349: Jones's comment | 350: Maltbie's response | What 693 and 347 visibly show |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Jones asks why Al-Khattab/UBL information will be added later if needed for the foreign-power connection; he fears setting the initial review up for failure. | Foreign-power material is being assembled and will be added if the package reaches a possible OIPR stage. | 693 p.5 visibly contains named leader/association passages despite its August24 face date. 347 p.8 has redacted blocks in the corresponding region. |
+| 2 | Jones objects to reported change from preparing himself to fight to train together in defensive tactics; he says neither interviewee used defensive tactics and calls the change misleading/weakening. | Verbatim interview answers are said unnecessary; sender considers the presentation accurate. | 693 p.4 first bullet and 347 p.7 first bullet both retain preparing himself to fight. That differs from the changed wording Jones describes. |
+| 3 | Jones says an edit made after their telephone conversation may misstate the response about hearing a plan; he reports confirmation by the interviewers. | Sender criticizes the earlier explanation and favors his own draft. | 693 p.4 second bullet and 347 p.7 second bullet retain the account of keeping himself from actually hearing/understanding. The actual disputed replacement and source interview are not supplied. |
+| 4 | Jones flags an apparently missing not, but also objects that an explanation was given and was unconvincing, rather than no explanation being offered; he argues the joyride context matters. | Sender reports inserting not and viewpoint wording, calls subject a liar and discounts joyride material. | 693 p.4 fourth bullet and 347 p.7 fourth bullet say unable to give a convincing explanation and retain the joyride discussion. They do not show the exact would/would-not sentence quoted by Jones. |
+| 5 | Jones distinguishes attempted but inadequate explanation from would not explain; he agrees with deleting Minneapolis opines while requesting relevant employment context. | Words added to cover point; remainder said not to add to package. | 693 p.4 fifth bullet and 347 p.7 fifth bullet retain no convincing explanation, employment context and Minneapolis opines. This visible state is not simply the deletion discussed in the emails. |
+| 6 | Jones qualifies the omitted first name as possibly insignificant, but calls the distinction between generally dangerous and the dangerous one significant to the source account. | Name/spelling attributed to ALAT-Paris; group connection judged adequate. | 693 p.5 visibly includes the dangerous one, with associated identities still redacted. The comparable 347 p.8 region is redacted; its exact concealed wording is unknown. |
+
+The most consequential new qualification concerns the word **not**. Jones's item 4 does more than flag a possible missing negation: he also distinguishes **offering an unconvincing explanation** from **offering no explanation**. Maltbie's acknowledgment that he inserted not cannot, by itself, establish whether the revised wording preserved that substantive distinction. The held 693/347 clauses can be pinned; the exact reported would/would-not sentence and resulting attachment still cannot be verified.
+
+Jones also says in item 3 that a questioned change followed their telephone conversation, and in item 5 agrees with one specific deletion. Item 6 qualifies the first-name omission as possibly insignificant. These limits remain beside his explicit possible-misstatement and misrepresentation concerns; the exchange should not be retold as either unanimous agreement or opposition to every edit. The interviewers' confirmation in item 3 is **Jones's reported confirmation**, not an original interview record independently examined here.
+
+Maltbie's [350](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/350.pdf), physical page 1, retains his accuracy assertion, conciseness/audience rationale, conditional OIPR stage and content-selection judgments. These are his explanations, not independent validation. Conversely, Jones's objections do not themselves prove an inaccurate final submission. The actual drafts, source interviews and materials reviewed by counsel are the discriminators.
+
+## Date, content and visibility questions remain separate
+
+**Visible foreign-link text versus proposed later addition.** The displayed August 24 LHM 693, physical page **5**, visibly contains named leader/association passages concerning Al-Khattab and Bin Laden. The August 28 quoted message in 349 proposes adding Al-Khattab/UBL foreign-power material later; 350 item 1 makes a possible OIPR stage conditional. The face date, readable content and proposed sequence remain side by side. They do not establish when this version was written, edited, sent or first known. Different draft states, a retained face date or copy/assembly history are untested possibilities, not selected explanations.
+
+**Redaction is not demonstrated omission.** In [347](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/347.pdf), physical page **8 / LHM page 5**, the comparable region contains substantial redactions. In 693 page **5**, more text is readable while individual/source identities remain redacted. This is a visibility difference between court copies; it does not prove that 693 added material absent from 347's original unredacted text, or that a particular field was removed from a package. No concealed text or identity is reconstructed by aligning the copies. The openly named figures in 349/693 are attributed source claims, not independently verified organizational relationships.
+
+**A six-page LHM is not automatically draft 2.** Both LHM counterparts have six pages, but page flow, front matter, readability/redactions and some displayed fields differ. The retained preparing-to-fight, joyride and Minneapolis-opines clauses in 693 do not simply instantiate the criticized replacement/deletion states described in 349. They might reflect an earlier, corrected or other state; an authenticated version chain is needed. We do not identify 693 as draft 1 or 2, declare the court index false, or select a preferred reconstructed document. The comparison is at selected clauses, not a complete character diff.
+
+The prior **Security/Surveillance** wording variation is also visible in 693: the opening request on page **1** says Foreign Intelligence Security Court, while the concluding request beginning on page **5** says Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Court. This is related-source wording, not a new independent finding that a different tribunal was involved. The prior 347 observation remains unchanged; no corrected value or legal consequence is assigned.
+
+## Relation to 351 and the authorization trail
+
+[351](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/351.pdf), physical page 1, quoted Maltbie first paragraph, reports the Bowman/NSLU meeting and a foreign-power-connection concern. The 349/350 exchange now provides a more specific preceding content/sequence dispute to test against it. It does not recover Bowman's own advice, the exact meeting package, an actual OIPR referral, a prosecutor's separate criminal-warrant analysis or a court disposition. The still-open adequate-group versus insufficient-foreign-power tension is retained without treating those terms as automatically identical or resolving the legal criterion.
+
+351's proposed further LEGAT work, Morrow's acknowledgment and the later reported non-pursuit/INS/France accounts remain qualified in the [preceding review](../moussaoui-fisa-draft-exchanges-2026-10-29/README.md). Nothing here independently establishes that all work stopped, that alternative work was executed, or that these related accounts independently corroborate wrongdoing. Displayed message times have no visible time zones or native transport evidence; no precise elapsed-time or reversal inference is made.
+
+## What has advanced, what remains TODO, and next feasible work
+
+The prior **DRAFT-T01** description of unacquired recipient questions has advanced: **349 now supplies the readable six comments in a court copy**. DRAFT-T02 now has precise reported paragraph targets and selected 693/347 clauses; DRAFT-T04 has additional readable association passages. The native full thread, actual attachments, original interviews and Paris/intelligence material are still different inputs. Do not keep calling these newly held source copies unacquired, or mark the wider dependent tests complete merely because part of an input was recovered.
+
+[OPEN-TODOS.json](OPEN-TODOS.json) carries all **DRAFT-T01–T06** identifiers and records each partial advance, specific remaining input, dependent test, completed search, next untried route and revisit trigger. All earlier gaps, the original **18 input requirements** and **five-track acceptance criteria**, including FAA correction/administrative dispositions, remain open. No prior source/report or public history has been rewritten.
+
+Next available source controls are court exhibit **32**, indexed as an August 17 witness affidavit, and **348**, indexed as an August 24 update exchange. Their bodies were not acquired/read in this unit. The affidavit may be a dated statement control; it must not be assumed to reproduce every earlier interview or the disputed draft. The update exchange may supply explicit version/routing pointers; its title alone proves none. Missing inputs defer dependent tests while these feasible independent source routes remain available; no outreach or new submission is authorized here.
+
+## Actual reading and preservation scope
+
